<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1585">1585</a>–<a href="/years/1636">1636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc was a Norman sea captain who founded the first permanent French colony in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. He landed on Saint Christopher in 1625 and, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-saint-christophe">Compagnie de Saint-Christophe</a>, shared the island with the English. In 1635 he claimed Martinique for the Compagnie des Îles d'Amérique, founding Saint-Pierre, and left his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-dyel-du-parquet">Jacques Dyel du Parquet</a> to govern it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc.jpg" alt="Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Théodore Gudin (1802-1880) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_fondation_de_la_Martinique_en_1635.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
